WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.276
A spreadsheet is a grid of cells.
Columns go down and rows go across.

2
00:00:09.496 --> 00:00:15.023
Column B, row 3 is cell B3. That
is how every cell gets its name.

3
00:00:15.523 --> 00:00:20.399
Here is the class pocket-money
sheet. Names, ages and pocket money.

4
00:00:20.619 --> 00:00:24.659
Each column has a letter
across the top: A, B and C.

5
00:00:24.879 --> 00:00:29.012
Each row has a number down the
side. One, two, three, four.

6
00:00:29.232 --> 00:00:32.854
A cell is named by its column
letter and then its row number.

7
00:00:33.074 --> 00:00:38.044
Column B, row 3 is cell B3.
It holds Sami's age: seven.

8
00:00:38.264 --> 00:00:42.861
Letter first, then number. B3
is a cell. 3B is not a cell.

9
00:00:43.361 --> 00:00:48.005
The bake-sale sheet. A cell's format
says what kind of thing it holds.

10
00:00:48.225 --> 00:00:53.333
Column B holds dates. Format it as
date and the sheet can sort them.

11
00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:58.523
Column C holds money. Format it as
currency to show pounds and pence.

12
00:00:58.743 --> 00:01:02.458
Cell D4 holds the word
Books, and a word is text.

13
00:01:02.678 --> 00:01:08.204
Format everything as text and the
sheet cannot sort dates or add money.

14
00:01:08.704 --> 00:01:14.602
Six children, one row each, with
their pet, their age and their club.

15
00:01:14.822 --> 00:01:19.234
A filter selects the rows that
share a characteristic, in a blink.

16
00:01:19.454 --> 00:01:24.005
Who has a dog? Choose the field
pet, choose dog, and press Filter.

17
00:01:24.225 --> 00:01:28.404
Zara's row and Omar's row light
up. Two children have a dog.

18
00:01:28.624 --> 00:01:35.265
Now filter age, more than 7. Four
rows match: Amal, Zara, Leo, Nora.

19
00:01:35.765 --> 00:01:40.084
Typing a value into a cell is
recording data on a computing device.

20
00:01:40.304 --> 00:01:45.459
Zara gets four pounds. Tap cell C4
on the tablet and put a 4 in it.

21
00:01:45.679 --> 00:01:51.159
Some data is a number you count.
Some is a word that says which kind.

22
00:01:51.379 --> 00:01:56.859
Cell C4 holds a number. Cell
A4 holds a word. Both are data.

23
00:01:57.079 --> 00:02:01.723
Now the sheet can do the work. Format
it, filter it, sort it, add it up.

24
00:02:01.943 --> 00:02:05.890
That is why so much of the world's
data lives in spreadsheets.

25
00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:11.684
One cell has one name. A range is a
block of cells from one to another.

26
00:02:11.904 --> 00:02:18.081
You write it with a colon. C2 to C4
means every cell from C2 down to C4.

27
00:02:18.301 --> 00:02:23.038
Select the range and format all
three cells as currency in one go.

28
00:02:23.258 --> 00:02:28.180
Ask for the total. Eighteen, twenty-five
and thirty make seventy-three.

29
00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.184
Change the thirty to forty and the
total changes on its own: eighty-three.

30
00:02:33.404 --> 00:02:37.583
A paper table cannot do that.
The answer keeps itself right.

31
00:02:38.083 --> 00:02:41.799
A cell has a column letter and a
row number, and data goes in it.

32
00:02:42.019 --> 00:02:46.848
Format each cell for its purpose,
and filter to pick the rows you need.

33
00:02:47.068 --> 00:02:52.455
Select a range, ask for the total,
and the sheet keeps the answer right.
